# For this project, I was tasked with creating the entire workflow for the data, starting from the extraction of the raw data up to the completion of the dataset. At the outset, I began with extracting the tabulated data available in two USDA NASS Honey Bee Colonies reports published in August 2024 and August 2025 through the use of Python library pdfplumber. This data consisted of the total number of colonies, maximum colonies, lost colonies, additions to the colonies, renovations to colonies, stressors to the bee colonies (varroa mites percentage, other pests, diseases, pesticides, and unknown causes), and CCD losses for each state per quarter. All the tabulated data was exported to CSV files, where after I filtered the data to be used just for the year 2024. Next, I merged the air quality data from the daily monitoring done by EPA using (All_pollutant_dataframes.csv, containing about 1.7 million observations daily with respect to five pollutants: PM2.5, CO, NO2, Ozone, and SO2). This was done through merging the daily data based on each daily observation to USDA NASS quarters and taking the average for each quarter, then doing an inner join of the honey bees database based on the columns of State and Quarter. The merged dataset consists of 128 observations with 22 features covering 44 United States states over four quarters of 2024. All steps in data preparation were done within a Jupyter Notebook file (data_processing.ipynb) and submitted to the GitHub project repository.
